# ChunkLoadError After a Release (Blank Page, Fixed by Hard Refresh)

## 1. What is it?

`ChunkLoadError: Loading chunk 42 failed` means the app tried to download a small JS file it needed (a "chunk") and that file wasn't there. It's not a bug in your component code. It's a mismatch: the browser tab is running an old version of the app, but the server now only has the new version's files. Because the download fails, the page that was supposed to load never shows up — so the user just sees blank.

## 2. Why does this happen?

Angular splits your app into smaller JS files instead of one giant file. Each lazy-loaded route (like a "Reports" page) gets its own file, and that file's name includes a hash, like `chunk-42.8f3a1c2.js`. Here's the simple play-by-play:

- A user opens your app. Their browser loads the main file, which knows the Reports page lives in `chunk-42.8f3a1c2.js`.
- They leave the tab open. Meanwhile, you ship a new release. The new build makes a new file with a new hash, `chunk-42.9b7e0d1.js`, and the old file gets deleted from the server.
- The user, still on the old tab, clicks over to Reports. Their browser tries to fetch the OLD file — `chunk-42.8f3a1c2.js` — because that's still what their copy of the app remembers.
- That file is gone now, so the server says "not found." The download fails, nothing loads, and the page stays blank.
- A hard refresh fixes it because it throws away everything and starts over — it loads the current version of the app, which correctly points to files that actually exist.

## 3. How does it work?

- **When you build the app**: Angular creates a separate file per lazy route, each with a hash in the name.
- **When you deploy**: the new files go up, and the old ones usually get deleted or overwritten.
- **When the user navigates**: Angular's router tries to fetch the exact chunk file it thinks it needs.
- **When that fetch fails**: the browser gets a 404, the download fails, and this shows up as `ChunkLoadError` (or a similar "failed to fetch module" message). If nothing is watching for this, the app just quietly fails — no error message on screen, just a blank page.
- **The fix**: write a small piece of code that watches for errors, notices when one looks like a `ChunkLoadError`, and automatically reloads the page for the user — so they get the working, current version without having to figure out "hard refresh" themselves.
- **One important safety check**: only auto-reload once. If you don't add that check, and the reload doesn't actually fix things (say, the user's internet is just down), the page could try to reload over and over, forever. So the fix needs to remember "I already tried this" and only show a manual "please refresh" message the second time.

## 4. Example

**`chunk-error-handler.ts`** — watches for the error, reloads once, then just asks nicely after that:

```ts
import { ErrorHandler, Injectable, signal } from '@angular/core';

const RELOAD_FLAG_KEY = 'chunk-error-reload-attempted';

export function isChunkLoadError(error: unknown): boolean {
  const message = (error as any)?.message ?? String(error);
  return (
    (error as any)?.name === 'ChunkLoadError' ||
    /Loading chunk [\w.-]+ failed/i.test(message) ||
    /Failed to fetch dynamically imported module/i.test(message)
  );
}

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class ChunkErrorHandler implements ErrorHandler {
  // This log is just so the demo below can show what happened.
  // In a real app you'd usually just call window.location.reload() directly.
  log = signal<string[]>([]);

  handleError(error: unknown): void {
    if (!isChunkLoadError(error)) {
      console.error(error);
      return;
    }

    const alreadyTriedReload = sessionStorage.getItem(RELOAD_FLAG_KEY) === 'true';

    if (!alreadyTriedReload) {
      // First time this happens: remember that we tried, then reload.
      sessionStorage.setItem(RELOAD_FLAG_KEY, 'true');
      this.log.update(entries => [
        ...entries,
        'First time seeing this error -> reloading the page. In real code: window.location.reload().'
      ]);
    } else {
      // Already tried reloading once and it happened again — don't loop forever.
      this.log.update(entries => [
        ...entries,
        'Seeing this error again after already reloading once -> NOT reloading again. Show a "please refresh" message instead.'
      ]);
    }
  }
}
```

**How this connects to your real routes** (this is the actual setup you'd use in a real app):

```ts
// app.routes.ts
import { Routes } from '@angular/router';

export const routes: Routes = [
  { path: '', loadComponent: () => import('./home.component').then(m => m.HomeComponent) },
  { path: 'reports', loadComponent: () => import('./reports.component').then(m => m.ReportsComponent) }
];
```

```ts
// main.ts
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { provideRouter } from '@angular/router';
import { ErrorHandler } from '@angular/core';
import { AppComponent } from './app/app.component';
import { routes } from './app/app.routes';
import { ChunkErrorHandler } from './app/chunk-error-handler';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    provideRouter(routes),
    { provide: ErrorHandler, useClass: ChunkErrorHandler } // plug our handler in here
  ]
}).catch(err => console.error(err));
```

**`app.component.ts`** — this just fakes two chunk errors happening in a row, so you can see both outcomes (reload vs. don't-loop) without needing an actual broken deploy:

```ts
import { Component, OnInit, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { ChunkErrorHandler } from './chunk-error-handler';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    <div class="page">
      <h1>ChunkLoadError Demo</h1>
      <p>Pretending two "chunk failed to load" errors happened in a row, the way they would right after a release.</p>
      <ol class="log">
        @for (entry of handler.log(); track entry) {
          <li>{{ entry }}</li>
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  `
})
export class AppComponent implements OnInit {
  handler = inject(ChunkErrorHandler);

  ngOnInit(): void {
    sessionStorage.removeItem('chunk-error-reload-attempted'); // start fresh for the demo

    // Pretend this is the first time a user hits the error after a release.
    this.handler.handleError(Object.assign(new Error('Loading chunk 42 failed.'), { name: 'ChunkLoadError' }));

    // Pretend it happens again right after — this is where the "don't loop" check kicks in.
    this.handler.handleError(Object.assign(new Error('Loading chunk 17 failed.'), { name: 'ChunkLoadError' }));
  }
}
```

